1
00:00:00,110 --> 00:00:02,230
August 12th, the year 2000,

2
00:00:02,670 --> 00:00:06,370
the Barents Sea. The pride of the Russian Navy is running a practice attack,

3
00:00:06,670 --> 00:00:12,110
with 118 men on board. At half past 11 Moscow time,

4
00:00:12,510 --> 00:00:14,670
seismic needles jump in three countries.

5
00:00:15,310 --> 00:00:18,690
Something inside the Russian fleet's exercise area has just exploded.

6
00:00:20,110 --> 00:00:21,950
Two minutes and ten seconds later,

7
00:00:22,270 --> 00:00:26,770
they jump again. Harder. The second blast is picked up in Canada and in Alaska.

8
00:00:27,870 --> 00:00:30,990
The submarine they called Unsinkable is on the bottom.

9
00:00:31,530 --> 00:00:32,690
And in her ninth compartment,

10
00:00:33,230 --> 00:00:35,410
23 men are still alive in the dark,

11
00:00:35,690 --> 00:00:39,550
waiting for a rescue. For the next nine days,

12
00:00:40,050 --> 00:00:41,430
Russia tells the world a story.

13
00:00:42,250 --> 00:00:44,730
Radio contact. Air being pumped down.

14
00:00:45,110 --> 00:00:47,890
A rescue closing in. Almost none of it is true.

15
00:00:49,170 --> 00:00:52,150
When it is over, Moscow will say the crew died instantly.

16
00:00:52,150 --> 00:00:56,870
Then divers will bring up a dead officer with two soaked pages in his pocket.

17
00:00:58,410 --> 00:01:01,990
Pages that prove the men lived for hours and that the story was a lie.

18
00:01:02,770 --> 00:01:05,710
This is the black box breakdown of the submarine Kursk.

19
00:01:07,230 --> 00:01:08,530
This is black box breakdown.

20
00:01:10,090 --> 00:01:11,610
Russia said they died instantly.

21
00:01:12,410 --> 00:01:15,030
They didn't. Chapter one.

22
00:01:15,430 --> 00:01:16,990
The boat they called Unsinkable.

23
00:01:17,890 --> 00:01:19,890
In the last years of the Cold War,

24
00:01:20,290 --> 00:01:23,650
the Soviet Union built a class of submarine for exactly one job,

25
00:01:24,150 --> 00:01:25,690
killing American aircraft carriers.

26
00:01:27,390 --> 00:01:28,930
NATO called the class Oscar II.

27
00:01:29,410 --> 00:01:32,550
Each one ran near silent and stretched 154 meters.

28
00:01:33,210 --> 00:01:35,650
About two jumbo jets, nose to tail.

29
00:01:37,110 --> 00:01:38,810
Under the outer casing sat a second,

30
00:01:39,090 --> 00:01:40,970
thicker hull, and inside that,

31
00:01:41,430 --> 00:01:42,810
nine watertight compartments.

32
00:01:42,810 --> 00:01:47,250
Her designers claimed a boat like this could survive a direct torpedo hit.

33
00:01:47,630 --> 00:01:48,870
The press called her Unsinkable.

34
00:01:50,730 --> 00:01:52,390
This is her sister ship, Aural.

35
00:01:53,010 --> 00:01:57,750
Same class, same yard. The Kursk herself was welded together at Sevardvinsk,

36
00:01:58,110 --> 00:01:59,710
commissioned in December 1994,

37
00:02:00,350 --> 00:02:03,330
and named after the greatest tank battle of the Second World War.

38
00:02:04,630 --> 00:02:06,190
Her main weapon was the granite,

39
00:02:06,670 --> 00:02:09,030
a cruise missile the size of a small aircraft.

40
00:02:09,030 --> 00:02:13,290
Twenty -four of them in angled tubes between the two hulls.

41
00:02:13,850 --> 00:02:16,570
This is a real launch, filmed aboard her sister ship,

42
00:02:16,730 --> 00:02:21,210
Tomsk. In 1999, under Captain Gennady Lyakin,

43
00:02:21,630 --> 00:02:25,710
the Kursk shadowed the American 6th Fleet in the Mediterranean during the Kosovo War.

44
00:02:26,250 --> 00:02:29,190
She came home celebrated as the best boat in the Northern Fleet.

45
00:02:30,730 --> 00:02:32,410
According to post -disaster reporting,

46
00:02:32,830 --> 00:02:36,210
her officers disabled the emergency rescue buoy on that same deployment.

47
00:02:36,210 --> 00:02:39,870
Afraid it might pop loose and betray her position to the Americans.

48
00:02:40,810 --> 00:02:44,290
It was never re -armed. Chapter 2.

49
00:02:44,710 --> 00:02:48,830
A Navy Running on Empty Her home was Vidyayevo,

50
00:02:49,070 --> 00:02:51,690
a crumbling garrison town above the Arctic Circle.

51
00:02:52,350 --> 00:02:57,170
By 1998, sailors on Russia's nuclear submarines were going months without pay.

52
00:02:58,630 --> 00:03:00,170
The fleet around her was dissolving.

53
00:03:00,850 --> 00:03:02,730
In the five years after the Soviet collapse,

54
00:03:03,090 --> 00:03:08,110
the Navy lost roughly half its ships and over a hundred nuclear submarines sat rotting

55
00:03:08,110 --> 00:03:11,730
at their moorings. The rescue force went first.

56
00:03:12,150 --> 00:03:16,610
The dedicated rescue mother submarines were scrapped in the 90s and the Navy's best deep

57
00:03:16,610 --> 00:03:18,210
rescue vehicles had sat in a St.

58
00:03:18,610 --> 00:03:21,610
Petersburg yard since 1994 waiting for repair money.

59
00:03:23,110 --> 00:03:26,030
What remained were two aging Priz rescue capsules.

60
00:03:26,330 --> 00:03:31,210
This is the exact type. And a surface rescue ship that was really a 20 -year -old converted

61
00:03:31,210 --> 00:03:34,030
lumber carrier. And in her bow,

62
00:03:34,430 --> 00:03:36,650
the Kursk carried a bargain from another era.

63
00:03:37,030 --> 00:03:40,570
The Type 65 torpedo. 10 .7 meters long,

64
00:03:41,070 --> 00:03:43,970
five tons and driven by high test peroxide.

65
00:03:45,250 --> 00:03:50,430
The Royal Navy had abandoned peroxide torpedoes back in 1955 after one blew

66
00:03:50,430 --> 00:03:53,430
up inside the submarine Sidon and killed 13 men.

67
00:03:54,130 --> 00:03:55,430
Most Western navies followed.

68
00:03:56,090 --> 00:03:58,150
Russia kept them. The range was cheap.

69
00:03:59,110 --> 00:04:03,150
The practice torpedo aboard came from a batch of 10 made in 1990.

70
00:04:03,830 --> 00:04:06,350
Six of the 10 had been rejected for faulty welding.

71
00:04:06,950 --> 00:04:09,250
Practice weapons, investigators later wrote,

72
00:04:09,650 --> 00:04:10,790
simply got less scrutiny.

73
00:04:12,150 --> 00:04:13,670
Her crew had never fired one.

74
00:04:14,030 --> 00:04:15,570
Their last live torpedo shot,

75
00:04:15,770 --> 00:04:18,390
three years earlier, used a simpler battery weapon.

76
00:04:18,830 --> 00:04:23,130
The investigation later found their peroxide training was never verified and some training

77
00:04:23,130 --> 00:04:24,310
log signatures were faked.

78
00:04:25,490 --> 00:04:30,510
Then, in August of 2000, the Northern Fleet gathered for its biggest exercise since

79
00:04:30,510 --> 00:04:33,550
the Soviet Union fell. More than 30 warships,

80
00:04:33,970 --> 00:04:35,850
with the Kursk given the starring role.

81
00:04:37,210 --> 00:04:38,910
She sailed on August 10th,

82
00:04:39,130 --> 00:04:41,250
the 27th birthday of Dmitry Kalestnikov,

83
00:04:41,770 --> 00:04:43,070
the officer who ran her turbines.

84
00:04:43,770 --> 00:04:45,490
At home, he left his dog tag,

85
00:04:45,910 --> 00:04:48,350
his crucifix and a poem for his new wife.

86
00:04:50,290 --> 00:04:52,610
Chapter 3. Two minutes and ten seconds.

87
00:04:54,130 --> 00:04:58,150
August 12th. The fleet spreads across the Barents Sea and the flagship.

88
00:04:58,750 --> 00:05:03,450
The giant battle cruiser Peter the Great takes station as the Kursk's practice target.

89
00:05:04,910 --> 00:05:08,210
At 8 .51 that morning, the Kursk asks permission to fire.

90
00:05:08,910 --> 00:05:10,770
Fleet control answers with a single word.

91
00:05:11,270 --> 00:05:15,310
Dobro. Good. It is the last word anyone will ever send her.

92
00:05:16,670 --> 00:05:19,790
Investigators would conclude that inside torpedo tube 4,

93
00:05:20,150 --> 00:05:22,090
peroxide was seeping through a faulty weld.

94
00:05:22,610 --> 00:05:24,930
On contact with metal, it decomposes,

95
00:05:25,770 --> 00:05:27,550
expanding 5 ,000 times over.

96
00:05:28,090 --> 00:05:31,190
An unstoppable chemistry her crew was never trained to fight.

97
00:05:32,690 --> 00:05:34,550
This is the actual seismic record.

98
00:05:34,990 --> 00:05:39,390
At 11 .29 Moscow time, the torpedo's fuel explodes.

99
00:05:40,030 --> 00:05:44,350
Magnitude 1 .5, roughly 100 kilograms of high explosive.

100
00:05:44,870 --> 00:05:49,730
The Kursk keeps moving. Fire is now loose in her bow.

101
00:05:49,730 --> 00:05:52,350
For the next two minutes and ten seconds,

102
00:05:52,630 --> 00:05:54,310
the Kursk keeps driving forward,

103
00:05:54,770 --> 00:05:57,070
carrying that fire toward the rest of her torpedoes.

104
00:05:58,610 --> 00:06:01,670
Then the second trace. Magnitude 3 .5,

105
00:06:02,050 --> 00:06:05,190
an estimated five to seven warheads detonating together.

106
00:06:05,610 --> 00:06:06,850
It is picked up in Finnmark,

107
00:06:07,190 --> 00:06:09,770
in Spitzbergen, in Canada and in Alaska.

108
00:06:11,750 --> 00:06:13,370
None of this rests on Russian data.

109
00:06:13,850 --> 00:06:16,170
It sits in an official Norwegian government report.

110
00:06:16,170 --> 00:06:19,690
First explosion, 7 .29 and 50 seconds,

111
00:06:20,170 --> 00:06:22,390
Greenwich time, strength 1 .5.

112
00:06:22,810 --> 00:06:25,610
The second, quote, much larger explosion,

113
00:06:26,030 --> 00:06:29,810
3 .5. Aboard Peter the Great,

114
00:06:30,290 --> 00:06:34,030
sonar operators hear the detonation and the crew feels the hull shudder.

115
00:06:34,550 --> 00:06:38,650
They report it. The report is dismissed and the exercise sails on.

116
00:06:39,890 --> 00:06:43,710
The blast has gutted the first four compartments and everyone inside them.

117
00:06:43,710 --> 00:06:46,110
The Kursk plows bow first into the clay,

118
00:06:46,570 --> 00:06:48,730
108 meters down and goes still.

119
00:06:50,390 --> 00:06:52,430
One system works exactly as designed.

120
00:06:53,210 --> 00:06:56,690
Both nuclear reactors sense the catastrophe and shut themselves down.

121
00:06:57,330 --> 00:07:00,610
The engineers who built those reactors saved the Barents Sea.

122
00:07:01,330 --> 00:07:04,450
Nothing saved the bow. Chapter 4,

123
00:07:04,750 --> 00:07:07,990
23 men aft. In the stern,

124
00:07:08,390 --> 00:07:10,990
past the reactors, 23 men are still alive.

125
00:07:10,990 --> 00:07:15,050
Survivors from three compartments grope through the dark to compartment 9,

126
00:07:15,470 --> 00:07:16,710
the one with the escape hatch.

127
00:07:18,270 --> 00:07:21,090
Above them, 108 meters of near -freezing water.

128
00:07:21,510 --> 00:07:26,090
A free ascent from that depth without surface support is close to a death sentence.

129
00:07:26,470 --> 00:07:27,810
They choose to wait for rescue.

130
00:07:29,110 --> 00:07:34,050
The fleet doesn't know. The Kursk's firing window closes at half past one in the afternoon.

131
00:07:34,750 --> 00:07:38,570
Silence. And for hours, nobody treats the silence as an emergency.

132
00:07:39,750 --> 00:07:43,850
Not until 11 that night, close to 12 hours after the explosions,

133
00:07:44,710 --> 00:07:48,350
does the Northern Fleet finally declare an alert and stop the exercise.

134
00:07:49,930 --> 00:07:54,550
And by then, the men in compartment 9 had already started writing down exactly what was

135
00:07:54,550 --> 00:07:56,010
happening to them, we know,

136
00:07:56,330 --> 00:07:58,350
because of what divers would find in October.

137
00:08:00,230 --> 00:08:05,490
Chapter 5, What Moscow Said What happened next is best told

138
00:08:05,490 --> 00:08:08,210
side by side. What Moscow said out loud,

139
00:08:08,690 --> 00:08:10,930
against what the record now shows was true,

140
00:08:11,290 --> 00:08:15,110
hour by hour. Monday, two days later,

141
00:08:15,410 --> 00:08:18,410
the Navy finally speaks. It says there were,

142
00:08:18,510 --> 00:08:20,450
quote, minor technical difficulties.

143
00:08:20,870 --> 00:08:22,710
It says the accident happened on Sunday.

144
00:08:23,330 --> 00:08:28,170
It happened on Saturday. The Navy says it is in radio contact with the crew.

145
00:08:28,550 --> 00:08:30,530
There had been no radio contact since Dobro,

146
00:08:30,810 --> 00:08:34,070
not one transmission before or after the blasts.

147
00:08:35,430 --> 00:08:38,050
It says air is being pumped down to the survivors.

148
00:08:38,910 --> 00:08:40,270
Nothing had ever docked with the wreck.

149
00:08:40,830 --> 00:08:43,610
That night, the rescuers were still just filming it.

150
00:08:44,890 --> 00:08:46,430
It says rescuers can hear tapping,

151
00:08:46,890 --> 00:08:49,410
an SOS spelling the word water.

152
00:08:50,110 --> 00:08:53,990
Investigators would later conclude the recorded taps did not come from the Kursk at all.

153
00:08:54,350 --> 00:08:55,950
The families dispute that to this day.

154
00:08:57,250 --> 00:08:59,590
And the new president, three months into the job,

155
00:08:59,790 --> 00:09:01,290
stays on vacation in Sochi.

156
00:09:01,290 --> 00:09:04,070
State television shows him hosting a barbecue,

157
00:09:04,530 --> 00:09:07,310
roughly a day after his best submarine went silent.

158
00:09:08,750 --> 00:09:09,790
He would say of it later,

159
00:09:10,590 --> 00:09:12,010
I probably should have returned to Moscow,

160
00:09:12,670 --> 00:09:13,890
but nothing would have changed.

161
00:09:15,530 --> 00:09:19,490
Even the military's own newspaper printed a warning about peroxide torpedoes,

162
00:09:19,870 --> 00:09:23,910
and overnight, the piece was replaced with speculation about a foreign collision.

163
00:09:25,610 --> 00:09:27,670
Chapter six, the rescue that wasn't.

164
00:09:28,850 --> 00:09:30,710
The real rescue looked like this.

165
00:09:30,710 --> 00:09:35,890
On the second night, the capsule AS -34 found the wreck by crashing into

166
00:09:35,890 --> 00:09:38,150
it. The impact damaged the capsule.

167
00:09:39,590 --> 00:09:43,850
It tried again and again to lock onto the escape hatch and failed every time.

168
00:09:44,410 --> 00:09:45,730
Batteries died mid -attempt.

169
00:09:46,370 --> 00:09:48,490
Crews stripped its sister capsule for parts.

170
00:09:49,230 --> 00:09:50,990
Storms shut everything down for hours.

171
00:09:52,530 --> 00:09:55,130
Moscow's explanations are preserved in the Norwegian report.

172
00:09:55,530 --> 00:09:57,230
Bad weather, a damaged hatch,

173
00:09:57,430 --> 00:09:59,450
and a 60 -degree lean of the hull.

174
00:09:59,450 --> 00:10:01,530
When the wreck was finally surveyed,

175
00:10:02,030 --> 00:10:04,130
the lean measured about one and a half degrees.

176
00:10:06,690 --> 00:10:08,390
Meanwhile, Britain had already loaded this,

177
00:10:08,710 --> 00:10:10,810
the LR5 rescue submersible,

178
00:10:11,030 --> 00:10:14,770
onto a ship, and Norway had deep saturation divers standing by,

179
00:10:15,170 --> 00:10:16,550
six countries offered to help.

180
00:10:17,970 --> 00:10:20,310
Russia said no, day after day,

181
00:10:20,530 --> 00:10:23,350
for five days, while the tapping story ran on television,

182
00:10:23,770 --> 00:10:27,590
the offers sat refused. On August 17,

183
00:10:28,110 --> 00:10:30,390
Moscow finally said yes. This ship,

184
00:10:30,730 --> 00:10:34,830
the Normand Pioneer, was already steaming north with the LR5 aboard.

185
00:10:35,390 --> 00:10:37,050
She arrived on the evening of the 19th.

186
00:10:38,670 --> 00:10:42,030
Norwegian divers went down from the Seaway Eagle on the 20th,

187
00:10:42,030 --> 00:10:45,110
and reached the hatch Moscow had called two damage to open.

188
00:10:46,250 --> 00:10:49,170
They opened it the next morning with minimal difficulty.

189
00:10:49,870 --> 00:10:52,810
Behind it, compartment nine was flooded to the ceiling.

190
00:10:52,810 --> 00:10:54,910
Nine days after the explosions,

191
00:10:55,370 --> 00:10:57,270
the rescue was over before it ever began.

192
00:10:59,150 --> 00:11:00,730
That evening, the fleet commander,

193
00:11:01,130 --> 00:11:02,870
Admiral Popov, went on television,

194
00:11:03,290 --> 00:11:07,730
removed his cap, and asked the mothers of Russia to forgive him for not bringing back

195
00:11:07,730 --> 00:11:13,310
their boys. The British commander who brought the LR5 chose his words carefully,

196
00:11:13,670 --> 00:11:16,410
years later. Given permission immediately,

197
00:11:16,910 --> 00:11:20,410
quote, maybe we could have been attempting a rescue about three days earlier.

198
00:11:21,710 --> 00:11:24,310
Chapter seven, Vidyayevo.

199
00:11:25,670 --> 00:11:27,510
Through all of it, the families were here.

200
00:11:28,230 --> 00:11:31,470
Vidyayevo, the closed garrison town the Kursk called home,

201
00:11:31,770 --> 00:11:35,830
being told day after day that the men below were breathing.

202
00:11:37,330 --> 00:11:39,350
At a briefing, Nadezhda Tylik,

203
00:11:39,730 --> 00:11:41,230
the mother of a lieutenant on board,

204
00:11:41,290 --> 00:11:43,130
stood up screaming at the officials.

205
00:11:43,730 --> 00:11:47,390
On camera, a nurse injected her with a sedative through her coat.

206
00:11:47,870 --> 00:11:49,390
She was carried out mid -sentence.

207
00:11:50,750 --> 00:11:52,870
Her husband said he had asked for it.

208
00:11:53,110 --> 00:11:55,970
Months later, she said that was a lie told to protect her.

209
00:11:56,310 --> 00:11:59,430
In her words, the injection was done to shut my mouth.

210
00:12:00,850 --> 00:12:04,110
On August 22nd, the president finally came north.

211
00:12:04,830 --> 00:12:06,410
These are the Kremlin's own photographs.

212
00:12:06,970 --> 00:12:08,070
Inside the officers' club,

213
00:12:08,530 --> 00:12:12,570
400 people, a meeting that ran for hours and a room full of fury.

214
00:12:14,190 --> 00:12:16,010
Mothers and widows screamed over him.

215
00:12:16,470 --> 00:12:17,990
Two journalists smuggled inside,

216
00:12:18,030 --> 00:12:19,790
heard one question again and again,

217
00:12:19,790 --> 00:12:22,010
why have you murdered our lads?

218
00:12:23,670 --> 00:12:26,390
He promised each family 10 years of an officer's salary,

219
00:12:26,670 --> 00:12:28,750
housing and education for the children.

220
00:12:29,190 --> 00:12:32,510
The state paid. The questions it never really answered.

221
00:12:33,930 --> 00:12:36,870
The next day, he answered questions on state television,

222
00:12:37,190 --> 00:12:40,190
calm and controlled. This is that actual interview.

223
00:12:41,690 --> 00:12:43,770
Two weeks later, on American television,

224
00:12:44,330 --> 00:12:46,190
Larry King asked him what happened to the submarine.

225
00:12:46,650 --> 00:12:49,170
He paused, gave a small smile and answered,

226
00:12:49,170 --> 00:12:54,490
it sank. Chapter 8, Two Pages

227
00:12:55,570 --> 00:13:00,110
In late October, a joint Norwegian and Russian dive team went back down to the wreck,

228
00:13:00,430 --> 00:13:02,610
this time to bring the men home.

229
00:13:03,990 --> 00:13:06,750
Warrant officer Sergei Schmeigen was the first man inside.

230
00:13:07,650 --> 00:13:11,510
Among the first four bodies raised that day was a captain lieutenant from the turbine

231
00:13:11,510 --> 00:13:13,990
room. In his pocket, two folded,

232
00:13:14,350 --> 00:13:19,350
water -soaked pages. The first entry is timed 1 .15

233
00:13:19,350 --> 00:13:23,530
in the afternoon, written in a steady hand while the emergency lights still worked.

234
00:13:24,150 --> 00:13:26,050
All personnel from sections 6,

235
00:13:26,370 --> 00:13:28,390
7 and 8 have moved to section 9.

236
00:13:28,710 --> 00:13:33,550
There are 23 of us here. The last entry is scrawled blind,

237
00:13:34,010 --> 00:13:36,470
in the dark. It is too dark to write here,

238
00:13:36,630 --> 00:13:39,130
but I will try by feel. There is no chance,

239
00:13:39,570 --> 00:13:43,450
10 to 20 percent. Let's hope that at least someone will read this.

240
00:13:45,010 --> 00:13:46,690
Then, for everyone at home,

241
00:13:47,250 --> 00:13:49,450
regards to everybody, no need to despair,

242
00:13:49,970 --> 00:13:51,670
and a signature, Kalestnikov.

243
00:13:53,710 --> 00:13:56,230
Remember what the Deputy Prime Minister had told the world?

244
00:13:56,850 --> 00:13:59,370
That almost everyone died before the boat hit the bottom.

245
00:14:00,050 --> 00:14:02,330
The note proved 23 men lived for hours.

246
00:14:02,950 --> 00:14:04,390
The official story was dead.

247
00:14:06,070 --> 00:14:07,250
Forensics filled in the rest.

248
00:14:07,930 --> 00:14:12,770
As the oxygen thinned, a chemical air regeneration cartridge touched oily water and flash

249
00:14:12,770 --> 00:14:16,150
burned the compartment. Carbon monoxide did the rest.

250
00:14:16,790 --> 00:14:19,950
The official finding, they lived six to eight hours at most.

251
00:14:21,410 --> 00:14:25,010
The family's lawyer spent years arguing the fleet's own logs said otherwise.

252
00:14:25,570 --> 00:14:28,230
Knocking, recorded from the seafloor into the second day.

253
00:14:28,750 --> 00:14:31,070
A military court refused to reopen the case.

254
00:14:31,750 --> 00:14:36,570
He fled Russia in 2007. A second note came up in November.

255
00:14:37,010 --> 00:14:38,750
Its author never officially confirmed.

256
00:14:38,750 --> 00:14:41,650
It described the gas and the rising pressure.

257
00:14:42,270 --> 00:14:43,590
We cannot last more than a day.

258
00:14:44,190 --> 00:14:45,990
The men's emergency breathing gear,

259
00:14:46,070 --> 00:14:47,870
it noted, was stored forward.

260
00:14:48,650 --> 00:14:53,270
Forward was gone. Dmitry Kalestnikov was buried in St Petersburg.

261
00:14:53,790 --> 00:14:56,130
Before sailing, he had left Olga a poem.

262
00:14:56,770 --> 00:14:57,910
When the hour comes to die,

263
00:14:58,170 --> 00:14:59,850
I want time to whisper one thing.

264
00:15:00,350 --> 00:15:05,410
My darling, I love you. The Navy never gave Olga

265
00:15:05,410 --> 00:15:06,390
the other side of his note.

266
00:15:06,390 --> 00:15:08,430
The part written only to her.

267
00:15:08,990 --> 00:15:14,110
Her words, there is something in that note they don't want me to see.

268
00:15:14,990 --> 00:15:18,590
Chapter 9, The Verdict A year later,

269
00:15:18,890 --> 00:15:21,390
Dutch salvage engineers did what many called impossible.

270
00:15:22,050 --> 00:15:24,530
Twenty -six cables, gripped straight through holes,

271
00:15:24,670 --> 00:15:28,770
cut in the hull, hauled 9 ,000 tons off the seabed in a single pull.

272
00:15:29,990 --> 00:15:32,530
They sawed off the shattered bow and left it on the bottom.

273
00:15:32,950 --> 00:15:34,950
The rest came up under the barge giant four.

274
00:15:34,950 --> 00:15:37,810
The heaviest object ever recovered from that depth.

275
00:15:39,070 --> 00:15:40,810
In this dry dock at Rosliakovo,

276
00:15:41,210 --> 00:15:45,230
investigators finally walked her decks and 94 more men came home.

277
00:15:45,890 --> 00:15:49,690
In the end, 115 of the 118 were recovered.

278
00:15:50,230 --> 00:15:53,930
Three never were. In July 2002,

279
00:15:54,410 --> 00:15:56,190
the prosecutor general announced the cause.

280
00:15:56,750 --> 00:16:00,970
High tests per oxide through a faulty weld in torpedo tube four.

281
00:16:01,430 --> 00:16:04,050
Exactly what the seismographs had said from day one.

282
00:16:04,950 --> 00:16:06,790
His own report described,

283
00:16:07,250 --> 00:16:09,290
quote, Stunning breaches of discipline,

284
00:16:09,910 --> 00:16:12,910
shoddy, obsolete and poorly maintained equipment.

285
00:16:13,790 --> 00:16:15,150
Then the file was classified.

286
00:16:15,870 --> 00:16:19,610
133 volumes. The public got four pages.

287
00:16:21,670 --> 00:16:25,210
Criminal charges, none. In the prosecutor's words,

288
00:16:25,610 --> 00:16:29,570
those who designed the torpedo couldn't foresee the possibility of its explosion.

289
00:16:30,390 --> 00:16:32,310
14 admirals and officers were removed.

290
00:16:32,830 --> 00:16:34,430
Officially, not as punishment.

291
00:16:35,510 --> 00:16:37,550
The counter story never died.

292
00:16:37,870 --> 00:16:40,870
A collision with a NATO submarine shadowing the exercise.

293
00:16:41,550 --> 00:16:44,130
One admiral called himself 80 % certain.

294
00:16:44,790 --> 00:16:46,470
No evidence was ever produced.

295
00:16:47,010 --> 00:16:49,150
And the seismic record shows an explosion,

296
00:16:49,510 --> 00:16:52,910
not a collision. 21 years later,

297
00:16:53,190 --> 00:16:55,970
the same fleet commander repeated it on state media,

298
00:16:56,190 --> 00:17:00,330
saying he could name the NATO submarine with 90 % probability,

299
00:17:00,710 --> 00:17:03,470
while admitting, quote, there is no evidence.

300
00:17:04,950 --> 00:17:06,850
Chapter 10, what the lie built.

301
00:17:08,310 --> 00:17:09,770
One more thing sank with the Kursk.

302
00:17:10,210 --> 00:17:11,670
Russia's biggest television channel,

303
00:17:11,910 --> 00:17:13,910
run by the oligarch Boris Berezovsky,

304
00:17:14,150 --> 00:17:15,990
had covered the disaster without mercy.

305
00:17:16,530 --> 00:17:18,590
The barbecue, the refused help,

306
00:17:19,190 --> 00:17:22,730
the sedated mother. The president was furious.

307
00:17:23,410 --> 00:17:24,750
Television people, he said,

308
00:17:24,990 --> 00:17:29,690
who had destroyed the very army and fleet were now exploiting this misfortune to gain

309
00:17:29,690 --> 00:17:32,870
political capital. Within months,

310
00:17:32,870 --> 00:17:35,290
Berezovsky was pressured into selling the channel,

311
00:17:35,690 --> 00:17:37,570
and his star anchor was taken off the air.

312
00:17:38,350 --> 00:17:39,950
Channel 1 became, in effect,

313
00:17:40,390 --> 00:17:44,570
the states. The first big domino in the takeover of Russian television.

314
00:17:46,110 --> 00:17:49,830
25 years on, that is how the anniversaries frame this story.

315
00:17:50,370 --> 00:17:53,690
Not as an accident, but in the words of the family's lawyer,

316
00:17:54,110 --> 00:17:55,730
as the place where the lies began.

317
00:17:57,310 --> 00:18:01,690
In 2009, a scrap dealer near Murmansk noticed something in his yard.

318
00:18:01,690 --> 00:18:05,830
The Kursk's actual sale, cut from the wreck and forgotten.

319
00:18:06,510 --> 00:18:08,050
The city raised it as a memorial.

320
00:18:09,570 --> 00:18:12,050
It stands there now, above the names of her men.

321
00:18:12,630 --> 00:18:14,410
To the sailors, the plaque reads,

322
00:18:14,950 --> 00:18:18,690
who died in peacetime. Machines fail.

323
00:18:19,250 --> 00:18:21,590
That's physics, and you can engineer against it.

324
00:18:22,030 --> 00:18:25,930
But almost everything that happened after 11 .29 that morning was a choice.

325
00:18:26,550 --> 00:18:29,750
And 23 men waited in the dark while those choices were made for them.

326
00:18:30,870 --> 00:18:33,970
The front of Kalessnikov's note has been read around the world.

327
00:18:34,470 --> 00:18:37,310
The other side, the part written only to his wife,

328
00:18:37,470 --> 00:18:38,990
has never been made public.

329
00:18:41,050 --> 00:18:43,230
If nine days of official lies got to you,

330
00:18:43,410 --> 00:18:47,150
wait until you see the plane that vanished with 239 people on board,

331
00:18:47,350 --> 00:18:49,430
and the search that is still empty -handed.

332
00:18:49,910 --> 00:18:51,550
That breakdown is on the channel now.

